1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,250
You see a headline. You
get angry. You share it.

2
00:00:03,973 --> 00:00:08,643
And only later do you ask yourself
whether it was actually true. Why?

3
00:00:08,973 --> 00:00:10,823
Why do we believe before we check?

4
00:00:11,463 --> 00:00:15,833
It would be comforting to blame the
internet for that. It isn't the internet.

5
00:00:16,683 --> 00:00:20,393
It starts with something your brain
does before the headline even lands.

6
00:00:22,213 --> 00:00:24,443
Imagine hearing a noise in the bushes.

7
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:28,743
You could stop and work out the
probability that it wants to eat you.

8
00:00:29,703 --> 00:00:33,533
Or you could run. Evolution
never waited for the results.

9
00:00:34,363 --> 00:00:36,593
If there was a predator,
the one who ran survived.

10
00:00:37,913 --> 00:00:41,903
If there wasn't, almost nothing
was lost. Fear was cheap.

11
00:00:42,353 --> 00:00:44,303
Being wrong about fear was expensive.

12
00:00:45,273 --> 00:00:48,603
So we ended up with a brain that
reacts first and checks afterwards.

13
00:00:49,553 --> 00:00:51,183
Only the bushes have changed.

14
00:00:52,313 --> 00:00:54,163
Instead of a tiger, a headline.

15
00:00:54,933 --> 00:00:58,323
Instead of thirty people in a valley,
a few billion, all within reach.

16
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:02,943
And that last change does
something the first one doesn't.

17
00:01:03,473 --> 00:01:07,343
One person becomes frightened.
Their neighbour sees it.

18
00:01:08,143 --> 00:01:11,523
"What's going on?" Now the
neighbour is nervous too.

19
00:01:11,603 --> 00:01:13,753
And somebody else sees the nervous people.

20
00:01:14,733 --> 00:01:17,363
Within a few minutes, a street
full of people is afraid.

21
00:01:18,353 --> 00:01:22,203
Notice what nobody in that
street got. New information.

22
00:01:23,293 --> 00:01:26,063
They saw other people being
afraid, and that was enough.

23
00:01:26,893 --> 00:01:31,983
It works the other way just as well.
Excitement spreads. So does certainty.

24
00:01:33,053 --> 00:01:34,803
A crowd doesn't only panic.

25
00:01:35,633 --> 00:01:39,043
It can also decide together that
something is going to be wonderful.

26
00:01:40,013 --> 00:01:42,983
And the more people believe it,
the more convincing it looks.

27
00:01:45,703 --> 00:01:48,233
Now hold that thought, and
think about an election.

28
00:01:49,143 --> 00:01:51,633
We like to picture it
as a rational process.

29
00:01:52,793 --> 00:01:55,083
Several parties, each with a programme.

30
00:01:56,053 --> 00:02:00,543
The voter studies the proposals, weighs up
the consequences, and picks the best one.

31
00:02:01,563 --> 00:02:04,473
How many voters have ever
finished a whole programme?

32
00:02:04,843 --> 00:02:07,473
How many check the arithmetic
behind the promises?

33
00:02:08,683 --> 00:02:12,433
And how many simply think:
"I like her." "I trust him."

34
00:02:12,923 --> 00:02:14,313
"That one seems confident."

35
00:02:15,043 --> 00:02:16,613
"I can't stand that man."

36
00:02:17,413 --> 00:02:21,963
In 2005, a group of researchers
tested how far that goes.

37
00:02:22,453 --> 00:02:26,543
They showed people photographs of two
candidates they had never seen before.

38
00:02:27,613 --> 00:02:30,203
No names. No parties. No policies.

39
00:02:31,213 --> 00:02:33,243
One second of looking, and one question:

40
00:02:33,433 --> 00:02:35,783
which of these two faces
looks more competent?

41
00:02:37,063 --> 00:02:38,643
Those snap judgements picked the winner in

42
00:02:38,723 --> 00:02:41,513
roughly seven out of ten
American Senate races.

43
00:02:43,023 --> 00:02:45,513
One second. A face. Nothing else.

44
00:02:45,983 --> 00:02:49,613
We don't even need to hear someone
speak before we start judging them.

45
00:02:52,323 --> 00:02:55,553
Which means the same sentence,
delivered calmly and steadily...

46
00:02:55,743 --> 00:02:59,513
...or delivered nervously, is
not really the same sentence.

47
00:03:00,613 --> 00:03:02,403
The information can be identical.

48
00:03:03,313 --> 00:03:04,723
Our reaction isn't.

49
00:03:05,273 --> 00:03:08,663
And now put that machinery in
front of something built to use it.

50
00:03:10,413 --> 00:03:13,523
We could say that people believe
nonsense because they're stupid.

51
00:03:14,453 --> 00:03:16,783
That explanation is far too easy.

52
00:03:17,573 --> 00:03:20,643
A false story doesn't have to
win by having better arguments.

53
00:03:21,913 --> 00:03:26,383
It can win by reaching the right
feeling first. Somebody is frightened?

54
00:03:27,153 --> 00:03:28,823
They are coming for what you have.

55
00:03:29,573 --> 00:03:34,883
Somebody is angry? Here is exactly who
did it to you. Somebody feels ignored?

56
00:03:34,973 --> 00:03:36,343
Everyone lies to you.

57
00:03:36,753 --> 00:03:38,503
We are the only ones who don't.

58
00:03:39,213 --> 00:03:41,333
And a story that lands on
a feeling gets clicked,

59
00:03:41,413 --> 00:03:43,863
and shared, and put in
front of more people.

60
00:03:44,273 --> 00:03:46,473
Which pays whoever published it.

61
00:03:46,553 --> 00:03:48,603
So more of it gets made.

62
00:03:51,053 --> 00:03:52,983
So that's our answer, isn't it.

63
00:03:53,553 --> 00:03:57,263
Emotions are faster than reason,
and the modern world knows it.

64
00:04:00,063 --> 00:04:01,263
But wait.

65
00:04:02,607 --> 00:04:04,787
If our feelings push
us around this easily,

66
00:04:04,867 --> 00:04:06,657
what are we supposed to do about it?

67
00:04:07,407 --> 00:04:09,217
Stop trusting our own judgement?

68
00:04:10,247 --> 00:04:11,857
Check everything ourselves?

69
00:04:12,547 --> 00:04:15,117
That sounds right until
you try it exactly once.

70
00:04:16,297 --> 00:04:19,767
Pick one claim about
the economy. Just one.

71
00:04:20,457 --> 00:04:21,807
Find the original data.

72
00:04:22,597 --> 00:04:24,527
Find out who collected it, and how.

73
00:04:25,407 --> 00:04:29,257
Understand the statistics well enough
to know what that number cannot say.

74
00:04:29,927 --> 00:04:32,357
Find the studies that
came out the other way.

75
00:04:33,397 --> 00:04:35,837
And then work out whether the
people who study this actually

76
00:04:35,917 --> 00:04:39,507
disagree with each other,
or only sound as if they do.

77
00:04:40,667 --> 00:04:45,237
That is one claim. Now do medicine.
Energy. Law. Climate. History. Finance.

78
00:04:45,967 --> 00:04:48,117
Nobody has that many lifetimes.

79
00:04:49,714 --> 00:04:52,484
So the real question was
never how to check everything.

80
00:04:53,354 --> 00:04:55,944
It's how to decide who
to trust when we can't.

81
00:04:58,674 --> 00:05:01,204
And needing to trust
somebody is not a weakness.

82
00:05:02,114 --> 00:05:05,204
When your car stops working, you
don't become a mechanic first.

83
00:05:06,334 --> 00:05:09,824
When the power goes out, you don't
sit down and study the national grid.

84
00:05:10,414 --> 00:05:13,364
You rely on somebody who
has spent years on it.

85
00:05:14,124 --> 00:05:16,894
Which is why the sentence
"I don't trust scientists"

86
00:05:17,044 --> 00:05:19,714
deserves a proper answer,
and not an eye roll.

87
00:05:20,794 --> 00:05:22,764
Because the worry behind it is fair.

88
00:05:23,414 --> 00:05:27,224
Scientists are not infallible.
Studies get overturned.

89
00:05:28,294 --> 00:05:31,064
Whole fields have been
wrong for decades at a time.

90
00:05:31,714 --> 00:05:34,604
And when better evidence
arrives, the answer changes.

91
00:05:35,854 --> 00:05:37,484
That isn't science failing.

92
00:05:38,234 --> 00:05:40,764
That is the only thing that
makes it worth anything.

93
00:05:41,944 --> 00:05:44,454
It also tells you what
we are really trusting.

94
00:05:44,664 --> 00:05:47,174
Not a person who cannot be wrong.

95
00:05:47,664 --> 00:05:50,834
A process that is built to
catch the people who are.

96
00:05:53,104 --> 00:05:55,234
But watch what usually happens next.

97
00:05:55,764 --> 00:05:57,814
Somebody says they don't
trust the experts...

98
00:05:58,764 --> 00:06:00,594
...and then believes a video instead.

99
00:06:01,564 --> 00:06:03,194
No evidence was examined.

100
00:06:03,324 --> 00:06:05,194
No field was studied.

101
00:06:05,404 --> 00:06:07,414
No better source was found.

102
00:06:07,664 --> 00:06:10,814
One authority was simply
swapped for a different one.

103
00:06:11,764 --> 00:06:14,394
Usually for the one that
says what we hoped to hear.

104
00:06:15,124 --> 00:06:16,534
That isn't critical thinking.

105
00:06:17,524 --> 00:06:20,214
Critical thinking never
meant "I believe nothing."

106
00:06:20,924 --> 00:06:23,514
It means "I want a good
reason to believe it."

107
00:06:25,214 --> 00:06:27,004
And here is the useful part.

108
00:06:27,654 --> 00:06:29,424
You usually can't judge the claim.

109
00:06:30,174 --> 00:06:33,064
But you can nearly always
judge the way it is being made.

110
00:06:34,174 --> 00:06:37,144
Does the source show you where
the information came from?

111
00:06:37,774 --> 00:06:40,444
Does it correct itself when
it turns out to be wrong?

112
00:06:41,384 --> 00:06:43,474
Does it tell you what
would change its mind?

113
00:06:44,204 --> 00:06:46,654
Does it ever say "this
part we don't know yet"?

114
00:06:47,494 --> 00:06:50,104
Try it on the next thing
that crosses your screen.

115
00:06:50,854 --> 00:06:52,464
A new study proves something.

116
00:06:52,974 --> 00:06:54,604
You can't evaluate the study.

117
00:06:55,684 --> 00:06:58,244
But you can ask whether anybody
has shown you the study at

118
00:06:58,324 --> 00:07:02,494
all, or only a screenshot of a
number with nobody's name on it.

119
00:07:03,584 --> 00:07:05,824
And a source that has
never once told you it got

120
00:07:05,904 --> 00:07:10,854
something wrong isn't more reliable
than the others. It just isn't checking.

121
00:07:12,024 --> 00:07:14,774
None of those questions
require you to be an expert.

122
00:07:15,544 --> 00:07:17,234
They only require you to look.

123
00:07:18,234 --> 00:07:22,624
It isn't proof. It is a far better
bet than how somebody made you feel.

124
00:07:25,344 --> 00:07:26,924
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

125
00:07:27,004 --> 00:07:29,754
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

126
00:07:30,604 --> 00:07:32,074
It really helps the channel.

127
00:07:33,864 --> 00:07:35,834
And now the uncomfortable part.

128
00:07:36,624 --> 00:07:39,694
You might expect that being clever
protects you from all of this.

129
00:07:40,684 --> 00:07:41,884
It doesn't.

130
00:07:43,265 --> 00:07:47,575
Researchers gave people a genuinely
difficult maths problem about some data.

131
00:07:48,585 --> 00:07:51,255
For half of them, the data
was about a skin cream.

132
00:07:52,355 --> 00:07:56,205
For the other half, exactly the same
numbers were about a law on guns.

133
00:07:57,515 --> 00:08:00,195
On the skin cream, the people who
were best with numbers did best.

134
00:08:00,275 --> 00:08:01,895
Obviously.

135
00:08:01,975 --> 00:08:04,745
On the gun law, they didn't.

136
00:08:05,335 --> 00:08:08,985
They split along political lines
harder than everybody else did.

137
00:08:10,725 --> 00:08:14,055
Being good at reasoning had not made
them better at finding the truth.

138
00:08:14,845 --> 00:08:17,675
It made them better at defending
what they already thought.

139
00:08:18,725 --> 00:08:20,615
That is the trap. Not stupidity.

140
00:08:21,405 --> 00:08:23,175
Skill, pointed the wrong way.

141
00:08:25,365 --> 00:08:29,595
It is also why "do your own research" can
leave you worse off than when you started.

142
00:08:30,365 --> 00:08:33,915
You go looking... and you
find what you hoped to find.

143
00:08:35,725 --> 00:08:40,455
And notice how easy it is to
enjoy that sentence. Those people.

144
00:08:41,325 --> 00:08:44,855
The ones who fall for propaganda.
The ones who share nonsense.

145
00:08:46,165 --> 00:08:47,365
Same brain.

146
00:08:49,904 --> 00:08:52,634
We also have opinions we
would prefer to be true.

147
00:08:53,304 --> 00:08:55,634
We also have people we
believe automatically.

148
00:08:57,174 --> 00:09:00,924
We can also be furious at a headline
before we have reached the end of it.

149
00:09:01,984 --> 00:09:05,774
So perhaps the question isn't whether
people are rational or emotional.

150
00:09:06,564 --> 00:09:07,934
We are obviously both.

151
00:09:08,974 --> 00:09:10,904
We built mathematics and rockets.

152
00:09:11,254 --> 00:09:15,104
And a single moment of fear can
still move us in under a second.

153
00:09:17,064 --> 00:09:19,854
Which leaves one small habit
that is worth the effort.

154
00:09:20,104 --> 00:09:22,734
When something makes you feel
strongly and immediately...

155
00:09:24,574 --> 00:09:26,404
...notice that the feeling arrived first.

156
00:09:27,154 --> 00:09:28,474
Then ask who is telling you this.

157
00:09:28,554 --> 00:09:30,484
And how they could possibly know.

158
00:09:31,454 --> 00:09:33,124
And then the hardest one of all.

159
00:09:33,814 --> 00:09:36,764
Do I have a reason to believe
this, or do I just want to?

160
00:09:38,624 --> 00:09:40,254
It costs about ten seconds.

161
00:09:40,404 --> 00:09:43,074
And sometimes, ten seconds is enough.

162
00:09:45,784 --> 00:09:48,174
Because "I don't know"
is a complete answer.

163
00:09:48,924 --> 00:09:51,994
It is a much better answer than
"I know, because I believe it."
